 "stimulus_html": "<figure id=\"Ch16_fig01\">\n<img src=\"exercises/media/4689499546899119.png\" alt=\"A chart shows the U.S. Population and Labor Force by gender and age for the years 1950 and 2000. In 1950, the male populations by ages are as follows: ages 0 to 4 just over 8 million, ages 5 to 9 about 5 million, ages 10 to 14 just under 6 million, ages 15 to 19 about 5 million, ages 20 to 24, almost 6 million, ages 25 to 29 just over 6 million, ages 30 to 34 just under 6 million, ages 35 to 39 about 5.5 million, ages 40 to 44 5 million, ages 45 to 49 around 4.5 million, ages 50 to 54 just over 4 million, ages 55 to 59 just under 4 million, ages 60 to 64 3 million, ages 65 to 69 about 2.5 million, ages 70 to 74 just under 2 million, ages 75 to 79 about 1 million, ages 80 to 84 about half a million, ages 85 and above under a million. In 1950, the female populations by ages are as follows: ages 0 to 4 just under 8 million, ages 5 to 9 over 6 million, ages 10 to 14 just over 5 million, ages 15 to 19 just over 5 million, ages 20 to 24, almost 6 million, ages 25 to 29 just over 6 million, ages 30 to 34 just under 6 million, ages 35 to 39 just under 6 million, ages 40 to 44 5 million, ages 45 to 49 just over 4 million, ages 50 to 54 4 million, ages 55 to 59 under 4 million, ages 60 to 64 3 million, ages 65 to 69 about 2.5 million, ages 70 to 74 just under 2 million, ages 75 to 79 about 1 million, ages 80 to 84 about half a million, ages 85 and above under a million. In 1950, the male labor force by ages are as follows: ages 15 to 19 just under 4 million, ages 20 to 24, about 5 million, ages 25 to 29 almost 6 million, ages 30 to 34 about 5.5 million, ages 35 to 39 about 5.5 million, ages 40 to 44 5 million, ages 45 to 49 just over 4 million, ages 50 to 54 just under 4 million, ages 55 to 59 just over 3 million, ages 60 to 64 over 2 million, ages 65 to 69 about 1.5 million, ages 70 to 74, ages 75 to 79, ages 80 to 84, and ages 85 and above, 0. In 1950, the female labor force by ages are as follows: ages 15 to 19 2 million, ages 20 to 24, about 2.5 million, ages 25 to 29 2 million, ages 30 to 34 2 million, ages 35 to 39 2 million, ages 40 to 44 2 million, ages 45 to 49 2 million, ages 50 to 54 about 1.5 million, ages 55 to 59 1 million, ages 60 to 64 half a million, ages 65 to 69 about a quarter of a million, ages 70 to 74, ages 75 to 79, ages 80 to 84, and ages 85 and above, 0. In 2000, the male populations by ages are as follows: ages 0 to 4 almost 10 million, ages 5 to 9 10 million, ages 10 to 14 just over 10 million, ages 15 to 19 just over 10 million, ages 20 to 24, just over 9 million, ages 25 to 29 9 million, ages 30 to 34 almost 10 million, ages 35 to 39 11 million, ages 40 to 44 just over 11 million, ages 45 to 49 just under 10 million, ages 50 to 54 just over 8 million, ages 55 to 59 just over 6 million, ages 60 to 64 5 million, ages 65 to 69 over 4 million, ages 70 to 74 just under 4 million, ages 75 to 79 about 3 million, ages 80 to 84 just under 2 million, ages 85 and over just above half a million. In 2000, the female populations by ages are as follows: ages 0 to 4 just over 9 million, ages 5 to 9 about 9.5 million, ages 10 to 14 about 9.5 million, ages 15 to 19 about 9.5 million, ages 20 to 24, 9 million, ages 25 to 29 9 million, ages 30 to 34 almost 10 million, ages 35 to 39 over 11 million, ages 40 to 44 11.5 million, ages 45 to 49 just over 10 million, ages 50 to 54 just under 9 million, ages 55 to 59 9 million, ages 60 to 64 5.5 million, ages 65 to 69 5 million, ages 70 to 74 just under 5 million, ages 75 to 79 just over 4 million, ages 80 to 84 3 million, ages 85 and over 3 million. In 2000, the male labor force by ages are as follows: ages 15 to 19 over 4 million, ages 20 to 24, almost 8 million, ages 25 to 29 8 million, ages 30 to 34 9 million, ages 35 to 39 10 million, ages 40 to 44 just over 10 million, ages 45 to 49 almost 9 million, ages 50 to 54 just over 7 million, ages 55 to 59 about 5 million, ages 60 to 64 just under 3 million, ages 65 to 69 just over 1 million, ages 70 to 74 about half a million, ages 75 to 79 less than a quarter of a million, ages 80 to 84 just over 0, and ages 85 and above, 0. In 2000, the female labor force by ages are as follows: ages 15 to 19 4 million, ages 20 to 24, just under 7 million, ages 25 to 29 7 million, ages 30 to 34 7.5 million, ages 35 to 39 8.5 million, ages 40 to 44 9 million, ages 45 to 49 8 million, ages 50 to 54 over 6 million, ages 55 to 59 4 million, ages 60 to 64 just over 2 million, ages 65 to 69 just under 1 million, ages 70 to 74 about a quarter of a million, ages 75 to 79 just over 0, ages 80 to 84 and ages 85 and above, 0.\">\n<figcaption>This chart shows the U.S. population and the labor force in 1950 and 2000 by age and gender. (credit: “U.S. Population and Labor Force, 1950 and 2000” by Bill of Rights Institute/Flickr, CC BY 4.0)</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><i>Refer to the charts provided.</i></p>\n",
